  "description": "#5140 Phase 3b — `paste_blocks` parses clipboard text (or takes HTML-paste blocks) and creates the blocks right after the anchor, in one transaction. Covers only what the tauri mock models: an outline whose bullet carries a continuation line and a nested child; two top-level bullets landing in order at the anchor's slot + 1 + k, ahead of the anchor's later siblings; text that does not open with a bullet, read by the same grammar (#5160 Phase 2a: consecutive lines are one paragraph block, a blank line separates blocks, `*` bullets nest by their content column; a heading owning its section is pinned by the mock's own tests, since `#` is a token separator here); a `blocks` input whose two-line block stays ONE block and whose depth-1 entry nests under it; #5160 D4 splices, a paste into the anchor's text (`splice`): mid-block with the first block's child following the anchor's own and `after` ending the last block, a one-block paste that only edits the anchor, `blocks` into an empty block, a first block joined after text as the text it was pasted as (marker, checkbox and property line kept as text, the anchor's own properties untouched), and a last block that is code, after which the text after the cursor is a block of its own; and the refusals. Names are left to the Rust tests, so no content here carries `[[` or `#`; task markers and property lines appear only after text, where both sides keep them as text. The returns pin every created row in document order. Seed: S1 (Home) > S2 (the anchor), S3, S4 > S5 (purged by an op), S6 > S7, S8 (empty), S9, S10. Re-author `expected` / `expected_ops` via CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1.",
